//@version=6
// MAR1 Gates, across the watchlist - four gate settings, two MA lengths each,
// on every pair at once.
//
// The companion of mar1Gates.pine, and the same eight machines: what that file
// draws on one chart, this one lays out for thirty.
//
// TWO TABLES, and a third that ships switched off:
//
//   THE LIST     mar1Scanner.pine's open table with a Gate column added. One
//                row per open slot, deepest first: what is running, what is
//                armed, and - as a faint wash - what is one step from opening.
//                This is THE table of this file. See ONE LIST below.
//   CLOSED       every window that finished inside the log window, with the
//                pair, the gate, both timestamps, the cost and the side. ONE
//                ROW PER EPISODE: windows that OVERLAP on one symbol are one
//                episode, because you cannot be in two positions on one pair,
//                and the one whose WINDOW OPENED first is kept - the same
//                machine mar1Gates.pine draws at full strength.
//   the grid     30 x 8, one cell per machine. An overview for setting the
//                thing up rather than for reading every minute, so it is OFF.
//
// The eight come out of maSweepPairs.pine, which measured the watchlist and gave
// a best and a second MA Length for each gate setting on each timeframe. The
// actual numbers live in the four gate input blocks below, not here - the gates
// are due another round of re-measuring, and a table in a comment would only be
// this file's stalest copy of them.
//
// EVERY CELL EITHER GATE CARRIES WAS MEASURED TOGETHER, pair by pair, timeframe
// by timeframe - the rule this file has held all along. A length that was never
// re-measured stays paired with its partner from whichever sweep last touched
// it.
//
// FOUR GATES, EIGHT MACHINES PER PAIR. A genuine fifth gate would need a fifth
// pair of shapes and there are only two plotshape styles left unused, both of
// which point - see THE EIGHT MARKS in mar1Gates.pine for the arithmetic.
//
// ONE LIST
// --------
// Running, armed and pre-armed are three states of one thing - a gate on its
// way through a window - and they belong on one list, sorted by how far along
// they are. The order that matters is DEPTH, and depth crosses all three.
//
// So there is one table, sorted running -> armed -> pre-armed and within each by
// entries. The state is a word in a column and a colour on the row.
//
// THE ROW IS THE LADDER. Its background is the rung, not the colour of one cell
// in it: a purple row is a window three entries past its anchor and you know
// that before reading a number. A pre-armed row is white at 88 - a wash - and
// that is what preparing for one looks like: nothing is open, nothing has cost
// anything, and it reaches no log, but the gate is one step away and it can be
// watched before it starts costing.
//
// A GRID AND A LIST WAS THE MISTAKE. The grid answers "which of a hundred and
// eighty machines are doing anything", which is asked once while setting up; the
// list answers "what do I act on", which is asked every minute. Two tables meant
// reading both and reconciling them by eye, and a scanner that has to be
// reconciled is not a scanner. The grid is still there behind a tick box, for
// the first question.
//
// THE MARKET COLUMN CAN ALSO SAY '~ entry' OR '~ warn' NOW, before ● IN ever
// appears - a LIVE PREVIEW of the candle still forming, read the same way
// mar1Gates.pine's own chart preview is: this bar's own trigger, projected
// through the gate without waiting for the close. It repaints, exactly like
// that preview does, and for the same reason - the candle it is read from is
// still moving. It only ever shows on a row that is already in this table
// for another reason; the grid, which shows every quiet machine too, does not
// carry it.
//
// ● IN ITSELF IS READ LIVE NOW TOO, and it was not always. It used to read
// the same firedNow the grid still uses - correct there, because the grid is
// only ever asked about the LAST CLOSED bar, but wrong here: firedNow is a
// `var` stamped once per bar at confirmation, so for the whole of the NEXT
// bar's formation it kept showing an entry that had already finished one
// candle earlier, on a bar that itself may have carried no rejection at all.
// IN now reads buyTaken / sellTaken straight - the same unconditional
// expressions mar1Gates.pine's own chart has always read for its Market
// column, which is why the chart never had this fault and the table did.
//
// In the list the GLYPH has a column to itself and is drawn one size larger than
// everything around it. Eight machines run on every symbol and it is the one cell
// that says which; at the size of its neighbours it was the easiest thing on the
// row to miss.
//
// ONE TIMEFRAME AT A TIME, AND WHY
// -------------------------------
// The chart's timeframe picks the column: the two timeframes are inputs at the
// top of the dialog - 1m and 3m by default - and matching the first runs the
// first eight lengths, matching the second the other eight. On anything else
// the tables draw nothing and say so. Both timeframes at once is not a
// decision, it is a ceiling -
// TradingView allows a script forty request.security calls, and thirty pairs on
// two timeframes is sixty. Open the script twice on two charts if both are
// wanted; the alternative was cutting the watchlist to twenty.
//
// THE GLYPHS - reading a cell
// ---------------------------
// A cell carries four things, and it borrows all four from the chart file so a
// row here and a marker there are the same fact seen twice:
//
//   GLYPH    which MACHINE ▲ ⬆ gate A  ● ■ gate B  ⚑ ◆ gate C  ☀ ❄ gate D - the
//                          SAME eight shapes mar1Gates.pine draws on the candles,
//                          first of each pair the best length, second the control
//   ARROW    direction    ▲ long, ▼ short - the side the open window is on
//   COLOUR   the ladder   white -> green -> yellow -> gold -> amber -> orange -> RED
//
// The chart and this table now use ONE alphabet rather than two that had to be
// translated. ▲ and ⬆ are directional and so is the side arrow beside them, so a
// short on those two machines shows a glyph and an arrow facing opposite ways -
// weighed and accepted, because the glyph is always first in a cell and the
// arrow always second. The column header carries the gate in brackets, so a cell
// never has to be decoded from memory at all.
//
// The number beside the arrow is how many entries the open window has already
// cost. That is the number a recovery ladder dies on, and the reason this
// scanner exists rather than an alert list.
//
// A cell with a background is a signal on the LAST CLOSED BAR: green behind it
// means an entry was taken, amber means a warning - the gate one step from
// opening. Everything else in the cell is a state that has been standing there.
//
// AND IT IS EXPENSIVE
// -------------------
// Thirty pairs times eight machines is two hundred and forty copies of MAR1's
// state machine walked over the whole loaded history. That is between the
// scanner and the sweeper in cost. Untick pairs first if it times out; the
// levers are the same ones and in the same order as in maSweepPairs.pine.
//
// KEEP THE SHARED SETTINGS IDENTICAL to maRejection.pine and to mar1Gates.pine.
// Only the three gate blocks are meant to differ between the files, and only in
// Percentage, Min entries and the MA lengths.
indicator('MAR1 Gates - scanner', overlay = true, max_bars_back = 200)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The grid
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Identical to the block of the same name in mar1Gates.pine, defaults included.
// The two files have to agree on all twenty-four numbers or the scanner is watching
// a set of gates the chart does not draw.
// NOTHING BELOW PRINTS ITSELF IN THE STATUS LINE, and that is what
// display = display.none on nearly every input in this file buys.
//
// TradingView prints an indicator's ARGUMENTS after its name in the legend, in
// declaration order. Four gate blocks are twenty-eight inputs on their own, and
// with the signal mirrors, the window and the table settings behind them the
// legend ran the whole width of the chart as a wall of bare numbers - '80 5 13
// 14 8 6 90 5 11 8 5 6 98 6 ...' - with nothing saying which number was
// which. A legend that has to be decoded is worse than no legend.
//
// display.none removes an input from that line and from the Data Window and
// changes NOTHING ELSE: the input is fully live, fully editable, and sits in the
// settings dialog exactly where it did. It is a display flag, not a switch, and
// it does not touch the saved-settings POSITION that everything else in these
// files has to be careful about.
//
// TWO ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT VISIBLE, and they are the two that can silently make
// every other number meaningless:
//
//   Streak gate    with Off there is no gate at all, the four blocks collapse
//                  onto each other, and every marker is an entry. Reading the
//                  tables without knowing this is reading fiction.
//   Fire signals   whether the hours filter is actually biting. A stretch of
//   only inside    candles with no markers means 'nothing triggered' when this
//   those hours    is off and 'nothing was allowed to' when it is on, and those
//                  are not the same fact.
//
// TO BRING ANY OTHER ONE BACK: delete ', display = display.none' from its line.
// To hide one of the two above: add it. There is nothing to keep in sync - the
// flag is per input and reaches no logic.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The timeframes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Which timeframe each COLUMN of the grid was measured on. First in the dialog,
// because everything below reads through them: every gate block carries two MA
// lengths per column, and the chart's timeframe picks the column by matching
// one of these two. On a chart timeframe that matches neither, the tables draw
// nothing and say so - the same behaviour the hard-wired 1m/3m pair always had.
//
// VISIBLE in the status line on purpose, like the streak gate and the hours
// filter: these two decide which set of lengths is live, and reading the tables
// without knowing that is reading fiction.
//
// The MA input NAMES below (maA11, maA31, ...) keep the 1 and 3 digits from
// when the columns were hard-wired to 1m and 3m. They are column positions
// now, not timeframes: the column's timeframe is whatever these two inputs
// hold.
//
// These two must match mar1Gates.pine, like everything else the two files
// share - the scanner watching one timeframe while the chart draws another is
// two files describing different mornings under one name.
gTF = 'Timeframes'
tfA = input.timeframe('1', 'First / second lengths column', inline = 'tf', group = gTF,
     tooltip = 'The timeframe each column of MA lengths was measured on. The chart timeframe picks the column: on the first of these two the first lengths run, on the second the second, and on anything else the tables draw nothing and say so. Defaults are the 1m and 2m the lengths below were measured on.')
tfB = input.timeframe('2', '', inline = 'tf', group = gTF)

gA = 'Gate A  ·  ▲ triangle + ⬆ arrow'
gB = 'Gate B  ·  ● circle + ■ square'
gC = 'Gate C  ·  ⚑ flag + ◆ diamond'
gD = 'Gate D  ·  ☀ sun + ❄ snowflake'

onA  = input.bool(true, 'Gate A on', group = gA, display = display.none)
pctA = input.float(55.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'ga', group = gA,
     tooltip = 'Gate A. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minA = input.int(6, '', minval = 1, inline = 'ga', group = gA, display = display.none)
maA11 = input.int(29, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a1', group = gA, display = display.none)
maA12 = input.int(24, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a1', group = gA, display = display.none)
maA31 = input.int(8, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a3', group = gA, display = display.none)
maA32 = input.int(19, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a3', group = gA, display = display.none)


onB  = input.bool(true, 'Gate B on', group = gB, display = display.none)
pctB = input.float(80.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'gb', group = gB,
     tooltip = 'Gate B. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minB = input.int(7, '', minval = 1, inline = 'gb', group = gB, display = display.none)
maB11 = input.int(6, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b1', group = gB, display = display.none)
maB12 = input.int(7, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b1', group = gB, display = display.none)
maB31 = input.int(13, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b3', group = gB, display = display.none)
maB32 = input.int(6, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b3', group = gB, display = display.none)

onC  = input.bool(true, 'Gate C on', group = gC, display = display.none)
pctC = input.float(90.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'gc', group = gC,
     tooltip = 'Gate C. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minC = input.int(8, '', minval = 1, inline = 'gc', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC11 = input.int(41, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c1', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC12 = input.int(24, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c1', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC31 = input.int(5, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c3', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC32 = input.int(6, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c3', group = gC, display = display.none)

onD  = input.bool(true, 'Gate D on', group = gD, display = display.none)
pctD = input.float(95.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'gd', group = gD,
     tooltip = 'Gate D. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minD = input.int(9, '', minval = 1, inline = 'gd', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD11 = input.int(9, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd1', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD12 = input.int(24, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd1', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD31 = input.int(9, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd3', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD32 = input.int(8, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd3', group = gD, display = display.none)

// WHETHER A SLOT IS TICKED - and until now this file DECLARED the four tick
// boxes and never read one of them. Four switches sitting in the dialog beside
// real settings, in the same block, looking exactly as functional as the numbers
// under them, wired to nothing at all. Unticking gate D changed the grid, the
// list and the log by nothing, and there was no way to find that out except by
// trying it and disbelieving the result.
//
// The reading is mar1Gates.pine's, exactly: the machines keep RUNNING, because
// there are two hundred and forty of them behind request.security calls that
// cannot be made conditional, and this switches off what they are allowed to
// SAY. Three places consume it below - the grid column, the live list and the
// closed log - and between them that is every output this file has.
slotOn(int s) => s < 2 ? onA : s < 4 ? onB : s < 6 ? onC : onD

// How many are live, for the footers. A table that has gone quiet because four
// gates are unticked must not read the same as one that has gone quiet because
// nothing is happening.
gatesLive = (onA ? 2 : 0) + (onB ? 2 : 0) + (onC ? 2 : 0) + (onD ? 2 : 0)

// Which column of the grid the chart is asking for.
//
// The two timeframes are the tfA / tfB inputs at the top of the dialog - they
// used to be hard-wired to 1m and 3m behind a TF3_ON constant, and the whole
// mechanism became these two inputs (2026-08-13, user request).
//
// The comparison runs through timeframe.in_seconds rather than string
// equality, so '60' and '1H' name the same column instead of accidentally
// missing each other. Both inputs are SIMPLE strings, so the lengths chosen
// from them stay simple ints - the only reason they can be handed to ta.ema at
// all.
//
// On a timeframe that matches neither, the first column's lengths still reach
// the moving averages, because a length has to be something, and tfOk stops
// the tables from reporting any of it. A length measured on one timeframe is
// not evidence about another.
//
// The same timeframe in both inputs is not an error: the first column simply
// wins the tie and the second one never runs.

// What the tables call the timeframes they accept. One string, so a message
// cannot claim a timeframe is welcome while the inputs say otherwise.
TF_LIVE = tfA + ' OR ' + tfB

secA  = timeframe.in_seconds(tfA)
secB  = timeframe.in_seconds(tfB)
tfIsB = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) == secB and secB != secA
tfOk  = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) == secA or tfIsB

lenA1 = tfIsB ? maA31 : maA11
lenA2 = tfIsB ? maA32 : maA12
lenB1 = tfIsB ? maB31 : maB11
lenB2 = tfIsB ? maB32 : maB12
lenC1 = tfIsB ? maC31 : maC11
lenC2 = tfIsB ? maC32 : maC12
lenD1 = tfIsB ? maD31 : maD11
lenD2 = tfIsB ? maD32 : maD12

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Signal settings - mirrors of maRejection.pine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gSig  = 'Signal (must match MAR1)'
gFilt = 'Filters (must match MAR1)'
gWin  = 'Closed log window'
gOut  = 'Tables'
gScan = 'Cost'

maType = input.string('EMA', 'MA Type', options = ['SMA', 'EMA', 'WMA', 'RMA'], group = gSig, display = display.none)

// A string rather than input.source, and the difference is not cosmetic. An
// input.source is bound to the series of the CHART's symbol, so passing one into
// request.security would feed every pair the price of whatever chart this script
// happens to sit on.
srcName = input.string('ohlc4', 'Source', options = ['close', 'open', 'high', 'low', 'hl2', 'hlc3', 'ohlc4'], group = gSig, display = display.none)

trendBars = input.int(3, 'Bars that must close on the trend side of MA', minval = 2, maxval = 10, group = gSig, display = display.none)
trendSep  = input.float(0.30, 'Min distance of trend closes from MA (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
wickRatio = input.float(0.60, 'Min wick share of candle range', minval = 0.30, maxval = 0.95, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
touchTol  = input.float(0.30, 'Almost-reached tolerance (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
atrLen    = input.int(3, 'ATR length', minval = 2, group = gSig, display = display.none)

allowBodyInside    = input.bool(true, 'Accept small body fully on the trend side', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowLongWick      = input.bool(true, 'Accept wick longer than its running average', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowCounterCandle = input.bool(true, 'Accept a single counter-trend candle', group = gSig, display = display.none)

// The two-candle module. Constants rather than inputs, for the reason spelled
// out in maRejection.pine. These three must match the block of the same name in
// the other files - nothing checks them for you.
DC_ON   = true
DC_BACK = 4
DC_REF  = 'Close'

// --- MODULE: flat filter ----------------------------------------------------
// A stretch where price walks a long way and arrives nowhere, and where this
// strategy spends entries for nothing: the trend test still passes on the
// micro-swings inside it, both directions fire within minutes of each other,
// and almost all of them fail.
//
// MEASURED AS AN EFFICIENCY RATIO - net displacement divided by the path
// actually walked, over flatLen bars. 1.0 is a straight line, 0.0 is a walk
// that ended where it started.
//
// Chosen over every width test - a narrow box, a Bollinger squeeze, a low ATR -
// because the flats that cost the most are NOT quiet. The candles are full
// size, they simply cancel each other out, and a width test looks at that and
// sees an ordinary market.
//
// PRICE ONLY, no moving average anywhere in it, and in THIS file that is what
// makes it affordable at all: the state machine below lives in pairGates, the
// shared block, so it is computed ONCE PER PAIR and all eight machines read the
// one verdict. Read it off an MA instead and thirty pairs carrying eight
// lengths each would be two hundred and forty flat verdicts about thirty
// stretches of price, at two hundred and forty times the cost.
//
// TWO THRESHOLDS, NOT ONE. It enters flat below flatLo and leaves only above
// flatHi, which is higher. With a single threshold the verdict flickers bar
// by bar around it and windows end up half blocked, which is worse than no
// filter at all.
//
// AND IT KEEPS STANDING ASIDE FOR flatWait BARS AFTER THE FLAT ENDS. The
// candle that breaks a range is exactly the wick-rejection candle this strategy
// likes, and it is also the one most often taken straight back.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO is close anything. It is wired into buyFound / sellFound,
// the same place the hours filter is wired into, so an open window keeps its
// entry count and its rung and simply stands still until the flat ends - the
// ladder is DEFERRED, never abandoned. That also means a winning signal cannot
// land during a flat, so a window cannot close inside one either. The machine
// is paused whole, exactly as it already is overnight.
//
// AND THE STREAK DOES NOT MOVE while it is paused, for free, because the
// grading reads buyFound / sellFound: a suppressed signal never reaches the
// histogram, so a flat cannot inflate the gate threshold or the failure
// distribution. Same behaviour the hours filter has always had.
//
// THE FIVE INPUTS ARE DECLARED DOWN BY THE PAIR LIST, after sym30 and before
// the request.security calls - not here, and not for the reason the two-candle
// module above gives. Thirty pair slots sit between here and pairGates()'s own
// definition line, and TradingView maps a saved setting onto an input by
// POSITION: declared here, these five would shift every one of the sixty pair
// inputs on any chart already carrying this script.
//
// Placing them past the pair list instead works only because pairGates() takes
// them as PARAMETERS rather than reading them as free globals - see its
// signature below. A parameter only has to be in scope at the CALL SITE, not at
// the function's own definition line, so the inputs can sit anywhere before the
// thirty request.security calls without pairGates() itself needing to move.
// scanBars and the two-candle module could not use this trick when they were
// written - they predate the pair list restructuring that made request.security
// calls a fixed block at the foot of the pair section - but there is no reason
// this module should pay a cost they no longer have to.
//
// flatOn = false restores the previous behaviour exactly.
//
// These five must stay identical in maRejection.pine, mar1Gates.pine,
// mar1GatesScanner.pine, mar1Scanner.pine, maSweep.pine and maSweepPairs.pine.
// Nothing checks that for you.
// --- MODULE: flat filter ------------------------------------------------ END

refLen = input.int(200, 'Average window (candles)', minval = 10, maxval = 200, group = gSig, display = display.none)

minPips              = input.float(1.0, 'Min wick length (pips)', minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
cooldown             = input.int(1, 'Min bars between signals', minval = 1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
perDirectionCooldown = input.bool(true, 'Cooldown per direction', group = gFilt, display = display.none)
gateMode             = input.string('Max of both', 'Streak gate', options = ['Off', 'Manual', 'Distribution', 'Max of both'], group = gFilt)

startHour       = input.int(4, "Start indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
endHour         = input.int(16, "End indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
restrictToHours = input.bool(true, 'Fire signals only inside those hours', group = gFilt)

successScope = input.string('Entries only', 'A window is closed by',
     options = ['Entries only', 'Entries and warnings', 'All found signals'], group = gFilt, display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Which stretch of time the closed log covers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Declared above the machine because the window decides what gets RECORDED, and
// the recording happens as the script walks past each bar. An input declared
// under the table would not exist yet when the first closure is written.
//
// Two questions, kept apart, exactly as mar1Scanner.pine splits them:
//
//   HOW LONG    a number of hours, or a calendar unit, or no limit at all
//   WHERE       rolling with the clock, pinned to an hour of today, or pinned
//               to a named day
//
// Every combination means something and it means the obvious thing: the date
// says WHERE the window starts, the length says HOW LONG it runs. A Monday
// chosen with Day gives that Monday, with Week gives that week, with Everything
// gives that Monday onwards.
//
// Day is the default. Two hundred and forty machines produce a long list, and a
// day of it is already more than one screen.
jrnLength = input.string('Day', 'Window length',
     options = ['1 hour', '2 hours', '4 hours', '8 hours', '12 hours', '16 hours', '24 hours', 'Custom', 'Day', 'Week', 'Everything'], group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Day and Week ignore the anchor below - they start at midnight UTC+1 and at Monday. 24 hours is not the same as Day: it rolls back a full day from now, across midnight. Custom reads its length from the hours field below.', display = display.none)

jrnAnchor = input.string('Ending now', 'Window anchored', options = ['Ending now', 'Starting at hour', 'Starting at date'], group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Ending now rolls with the clock. Starting at hour pins a stretch of TODAY. Starting at date pins a stretch of a named day, which is the only one that can read back a day that has already ended.', display = display.none)

jrnFromHour = input.int(9, 'Starting hour, UTC+1', minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Only used when the anchor above is Starting at hour and the length is in hours.', display = display.none)

// A float because a typed length is not a whole number of hours: 2.5 and 0.75
// are ordinary things to ask for, and rounding them to the hour would answer a
// question nobody asked.
jrnHours = input.float(3.0, 'Custom window length, hours', minval = 0.05, maxval = 168.0, step = 0.25, group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Used only when 'Window length' is Custom. Fractions are honoured - 2.5 is two and a half hours, 0.75 is forty-five minutes.", display = display.none)

// 1767225600000 is 2026-01-01 00:00 UTC in milliseconds, and it is a literal
// rather than a timestamp() call because input.time demands a CONST int and
// timestamp() does not give one - its numeric form reads the exchange timezone,
// which cannot be known at compile time. It is only where the picker opens.
jrnFrom = input.time(1767225600000, 'Custom window starts', group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Used only when 'Window anchored' is Starting at date. The window runs from here for the length above. The log's header prints the window that was actually applied, in UTC+1 - check it there rather than assuming.", display = display.none)

jrnToday = input.bool(false, 'Custom window starts today (ignores the date)', group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Overrides the date above with today's midnight, and follows the calendar - it still means today tomorrow.", display = display.none)

// Repeated as a constant so the header can tell a date that was CHOSEN from one
// that was never touched. Without it the only honest warning would be permanent.
JRN_FROM_DEFAULT = 1767225600000

// How many closures the log KEEPS, which is not the same question as how many it
// draws. Zero keeps everything the window holds, which is what makes the average
// on the header line describe the period the header names.
jrnCap = input.int(0, 'Keep at most N closures (0 = all)', minval = 0, maxval = 50000, group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Zero keeps every closure inside the window. Type a number only to cut the work when the script times out - the table draws what the block layout holds either way. How far back the log reaches is decided by Window length, not by this.', display = display.none)

// The ceiling that applies when the cap is 0: Pine refuses an array past 100,000
// elements and there are seven of them here. When it bites it says so on the
// header line rather than quietly dropping rows, which is the whole difference
// between a ceiling and a bug.
JRN_KEEP_MAX = 50000
jrnKeep = jrnCap <= 0 ? JRN_KEEP_MAX : jrnCap

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The tables
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gridPos = input.string('Top right', 'Grid position',
     options = ['Top right', 'Middle right', 'Bottom right', 'Top center', 'Middle center', 'Bottom center', 'Top left', 'Middle left', 'Bottom left'], group = gOut, display = display.none)
// Bottom right, which is where mar1Gates.pine puts its own RUNNING table. The
// two are read one after the other on the same screen and the eye should not
// have to find them in different corners.
runPos = input.string('Bottom right', 'Live list position',
     options = ['Bottom right', 'Middle right', 'Top right', 'Bottom center', 'Middle center', 'Bottom left', 'Middle left'], group = gOut, display = display.none)
logPos = input.string('Middle left', 'CLOSED log position',
     options = ['Middle left', 'Top left', 'Bottom left', 'Middle center', 'Bottom center', 'Top center'], group = gOut, display = display.none)

// ON by default, and it was off for a while on an argument that turned out to be
// about the FIRST version of this table rather than about this one. Two tables
// that had to be reconciled by eye was the fault; the grid answering a different
// question from the list is not. It opens top right, the list bottom right and
// the log middle left, so the three sit in three corners and none of them has to
// be found.
showGrid = input.bool(true, 'Also show the 30 x 8 grid', group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The overview: one row per pair, one column per machine, in the top right. It answers "which of the two hundred and forty are doing anything", which the live list below it cannot - that one is sorted by depth and cut at the shallow end. Untick to get the corner back.', display = display.none)
showRun  = input.bool(true, 'Show the live list', group = gOut, display = display.none)

// THREE STATES, not two, and it is mar1Scanner.pine's 'Show the log as' brought
// over unchanged - same three words, same order, same meaning.
//
//   Full list      the summary line and every row under it
//   Summary only   the summary line ALONE - the window, the closure count, the
//                  entries and the average, which is the number the table exists
//                  for and the one maSweepPairs.pine sweeps
//   Hidden         nothing on the chart
//
// A day of closures across thirty pairs is taller than the chart, and there was
// nothing between all of it and none of it. The middle setting is the one a
// working screen actually wants: one line that says what the day cost, without
// the table standing over the price it is supposed to be read against.
//
// THE LOG IS RECORDED WHATEVER THIS SAYS. Hidden is a drawing decision, not a
// collection one - the arrays are maintained on every bar either way, so
// switching back to Full list shows the whole window rather than only what has
// closed since the switch.
//
// It REPLACES a tick box at the same position rather than being appended, and
// that is deliberate. TradingView maps a saved setting onto an input by
// position, so swapping one input for another of a different TYPE costs exactly
// this one setting - it cannot take the old boolean, so it falls back to its own
// default - while every input after it keeps its index and its value. Appending
// a dropdown and leaving the tick box would have cost nothing at all, and would
// have left two controls for one thing.
showLog  = input.string('Summary only', 'Show the log as',
     options = ['Full list', 'Summary only', 'Hidden'], group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Summary only keeps the header line - the window, the closure count, the entries and the average - and drops every row under it. The log is still RECORDED under all three, so switching back to Full list shows the whole window rather than only what has closed since.', display = display.none)

txtSizeName = input.string('Tiny', 'Text size', options = ['Tiny', 'Small', 'Normal'], group = gOut, display = display.none)

// Rows for pairs with nothing happening on any of the eight. Off by default:
// thirty rows of dots is thirty rows of nothing, and the whole point of a grid
// this wide is that the eye lands on what is moving.
showQuiet = input.bool(false, 'Grid: keep rows where nothing is open', group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'A pair is quiet when no slot has a window open, no slot fired on the last bar and no gate is one step from opening. Off hides those rows and the footer says how many were hidden.', display = display.none)

// The RUNNING list is sorted deepest-first, so a cap here cuts the SHALLOW end -
// which is the end worth cutting. Without it a busy morning puts sixty rows on
// the chart and the deep ones are still at the top, just with fifty behind them.
// Rows are laid out in TWO side-by-side blocks (2026-08-12), so this number is
// split down the middle: the first half fills the left block top to bottom, the
// rest continues in the right one. An odd number leaves the right block one row
// short, which is drawn as a filled dark cell rather than a hole.
//
// THIRTY-TWO - sixteen per block - because twenty-one was cutting the whole
// pre-armed group off (user report, 2026-08-12: a live count of 16 RUNNING and
// 5 ARMED is exactly twenty-one, so all eight pre-armed rows fell past the cut
// while the header sat there saying '8 pre-armed'). Pre-armed is the one state
// worth seeing BEFORE it costs anything, and it sorts last by construction, so
// it is always the group a cap takes first. Sixteen rows tall is still shorter
// than the sixteen-row single column this replaced.
runMax = input.int(32, 'Live list: most rows to show', minval = 3, maxval = 40, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Sorted running first, then armed, then pre-armed, and within each by entries. Laid out in two columns, filled left block first. The cut is at the shallow end and the header says how many were left off.', display = display.none)

// How tall each block of the closed log is. 0 is auto: twenty tall until all
// four blocks are in use, then taller. Width is the direction a chart has spare.
logPerCol = input.int(0, 'Closed log: rows per column (0 = auto)', minval = 0, maxval = 60, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The list wraps into blocks side by side rather than growing down. 0 sizes the blocks itself: twenty tall until all four blocks are in use, then taller.', display = display.none)

// 0 - no limit - is the default, and for the same reason the other files give:
// the streak distribution is built from the ENTIRE loaded history, so a window
// here is a different sample, a different percentile and therefore a different
// gate threshold. It changes the answer rather than the cost.
scanBars = input.int(0, 'Bars of history per pair (0 = all)', minval = 0, maxval = 20000, group = gScan,
     tooltip = 'Leave at 0 so the gate is built from the same history the chart uses. A limited history gives a different gate threshold, and the states stop describing the strategy you trade.', display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The window, as two timestamps
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Everything is measured against the pair: what gets recorded, what gets aged
// out, and what the header claims. One rule rather than four mechanisms, which
// is what lets a rolling window, a calendar one and a named stretch of the
// morning share the same code.
//
// Built from timenow, the WALL CLOCK, and not from the bar's own time. That is
// the whole trick: timenow reads the same on a bar from last Tuesday as on the
// live one, so "is this closure inside the window" has an answer at every point
// in history - and the window is complete the moment the script loads rather
// than filling up only from when the tab was opened.
//
// UTC+1 for the calendar boundaries, the frame the hours filter and every
// timestamp in this project already use. Epoch day 0 was a Thursday, hence the 3
// in the week arithmetic: it puts the boundary on Monday.
utcShift = 3600000
dayMs    = 86400000
hourMs   = 3600000

nowDayIdx  = math.floor((timenow + utcShift) / dayMs)
nowWeekIdx = math.floor((nowDayIdx + 3) / 7)

todayStart = nowDayIdx * dayMs - utcShift
weekStart  = (7 * nowWeekIdx - 3) * dayMs - utcShift

// Zero means the length is a calendar unit rather than a count of hours, which
// is also what makes the anchor inapplicable - Day and Week already know where
// they start.
lengthHours = jrnLength == '1 hour' ? 1.0 :
     jrnLength == '2 hours' ? 2.0 :
     jrnLength == '4 hours' ? 4.0 :
     jrnLength == '8 hours' ? 8.0 :
     jrnLength == '12 hours' ? 12.0 :
     jrnLength == '16 hours' ? 16.0 :
     jrnLength == '24 hours' ? 24.0 :
     jrnLength == 'Custom' ? jrnHours :
     0.0

lengthMs = int(lengthHours * hourMs)

calMs = jrnLength == 'Day' ? dayMs :
     jrnLength == 'Week' ? 7 * dayMs :
     0

// What was TYPED versus what the window ends up being. Zero span is unbounded,
// which only Everything produces.
spanMs = lengthHours > 0 ? lengthMs : calMs

datePinned = jrnAnchor == 'Starting at date'
hourPinned = lengthHours > 0 and jrnAnchor == 'Starting at hour'
pinned     = datePinned or hourPinned

customStart = jrnToday ? todayStart : jrnFrom
pinFrom     = datePinned ? customStart : todayStart + jrnFromHour * hourMs

// The date anchor is read FIRST, so a calendar length cannot silently overrule
// it by picking its own start behind it.
periodFrom = datePinned ? customStart :
     jrnLength == 'Day' ? todayStart :
     jrnLength == 'Week' ? weekStart :
     jrnLength == 'Everything' ? 0 :
     hourPinned ? pinFrom :
     timenow - lengthMs

// A day past the wall clock is past everything that has closed, since a closure
// is a candle that has already ended. Only a BOUNDED pinned window has a real
// ceiling - without one, naming 09:00 for an hour would show the morning and
// everything after it.
periodTo = pinned and spanMs > 0 ? pinFrom + spanMs : timenow + dayMs

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The colour ladder, as a number and then as a colour
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Split in two because a colour cannot cross request.security. The step is
// computed inside the requested symbol's context and the hue is put back on
// this side, which is exactly what mar1Scanner.pine does - and these two
// functions are copies of ladderStep and stepColor there, required to stay
// identical to them and to streakColor in maRejection.pine.
//
//   1  limit - 1     white       one short, the gate is about to open
//   2  limit         #22C55E     entry 1 - bright green, a normal cost
//   3  limit + 1     #15803D     entry 2 - a visibly deeper green
//   4  limit + 2     #166534     entry 3 - the deepest green, still clearly green
//   5  +3            #EA580C     entry 4 - the jump: green ends here, hot starts
//   6  +4            #DC2626     entry 5 - a visibly hotter red
//   8  +5 beyond     #450A0A     entry 6 on - near-black red, a run to avoid
//   7  below         faded       carries nothing from this scale
//   0  no ladder     the gate is Off, or no anchor has been captured yet
//
// ONE SUCCESSIVE WARM GRADIENT (2026-08-10, user request), replacing a set of
// hues - lime, orange, lilac, purple, red - that read as six unrelated colours
// rather than six steps of one thing.
//
// FOURTH PASS moved the boundary itself: entries 1-3 are all SHADES OF GREEN
// rather than green fading into gold by entry 3, so the whole low end reads as
// one family and "still fine" all the way through the third entry. The jump to
// entry 4 is deliberately abrupt - orange lands right after the deepest green
// with nothing between them, so the fourth entry is where "fine" visibly
// stops. 5 moves to red and 6 and beyond is the one colour on the whole
// ladder that should read as "stop", carrying the same reduced-transparency
// "loud" treatment the old red step had.
//
// FIFTH PASS THE SAME DAY widens the SPREAD inside each half, because the
// fourth pass clustered all three greens in Tailwind's 600-800 band and all
// three reds in its 600-900 band, and three shades that close together were
// hard to tell apart at a glance even though they were, strictly, different
// colours. Each half now spans much further down its own scale - green from
// 500 to 900, red from 600 to 950 - so entry 1 vs 2 vs 3 and entry 4 vs 5 vs 6
// are each a real jump in lightness as well as in hue, not just a hue drift.
// The FAMILIES and the BOUNDARY are unchanged from the fourth pass; only how
// far apart the three members of each family sit.
//
// SIXTH PASS (2026-08-10, user report against the live chart) pulled entry 3
// back from #14532D to #166534: at the transparency the boxes and rows
// actually draw at, that dark a hex washes out rather than reading as a
// colour, which is what "the third one is very dull" meant in practice. Entry
// 2 moved from #16A34A to #15803D at the same time, so entry 1 vs 2 is a real
// jump instead of the closest pair on the whole ladder. Family and boundary
// are still unchanged from the fourth pass.
//
// PRIOR PASSES, for the record, on how loud/rich each step reads before the
// fourth pass changed the shape: first cut used Material's Accent shades
// (#64DD17, #AEEA00, #FFD600, #FF9100, #FF3D00, #D50000) - bright and thin,
// closer to highlighter ink than paint. Second moved to Material's 700-900
// shades (#2E7D32, #9E9D24, #F9A825, #EF6C00, #D84315, #B71C1C), which fixed
// the glare but read as pale - Material's own 700-900 tones mix grey into the
// hue as they darken. Third moved to Tailwind's 700/900 shades, which held
// saturation as they darkened.
//
// WHITE AT STEP 1 IS UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY OUTSIDE THIS GRADIENT - it is
// not a cost step at all, it is pre-armed: nothing has been risked yet, and
// the PRE-ARMED plate in the live list already owns that meaning. Folding it
// into the gradient's green end would say a slot that has not traded anything
// is "doing well", which is not what pre-armed means.
ladderStep(int streak, int limit) =>
    limit <= 0 ? 0 :
     streak == limit - 1 ? 1 :
     streak == limit ? 2 :
     streak > limit ? (streak - limit < 2 ? 3 : streak - limit < 3 ? 4 : streak - limit < 4 ? 5 :
          streak - limit < 5 ? 6 : 8) :
     7

// The rung a window of n entries is standing on, which is the colour its cells
// are already wearing. The ladder runs one step BEHIND the entry count - a
// window that took four entries reached +3 and is #EA580C, one that took five
// reached +4 and is #DC2626 - so this is n + 1, held at the top step, and past
// five entries it is the #450A0A step 8 rather than a colour that keeps
// climbing forever.
depthStep(int n) =>
    n <= 0 ? 0 : n >= 6 ? 8 : n + 1

stepColor(int step) =>
    step == 1 ? color.white :
     step == 2 ? #22C55E :
     step == 3 ? #15803D :
     step == 4 ? #166534 :
     step == 5 ? #EA580C :
     step == 6 ? #DC2626 :
     step == 8 ? #450A0A :
     step == 7 ? color.new(color.silver, 55) :
     color.new(#777777, 40)

// Index at which the cumulative distribution of the streak histogram crosses
// perc. Identical to calculateIndex in maRejection.pine, including the inclusive
// comparison - a strict one slides the answer one bucket to the right.
calcIndex(int[] arr, float perc) =>
    total = 0.0
    for i = 1 to array.size(arr) - 1
        total += array.get(arr, i)
    if total == 0
        -1
    else
        cumSum = 0.0
        index = -1
        for i = 1 to array.size(arr) - 1
            cumSum += array.get(arr, i) / total * 100
            if cumSum >= perc and index == -1
                index := i
                break
        index

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two packed numbers per slot
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each pair has to hand back what it is doing NOW and what it last FINISHED,
// for eight slots. As plain tuple members that is twelve fields times eight times
// thirty - nearly three thousand names in a scope that allows a thousand. Packed,
// it is two numbers per slot and four hundred and eighty names.
//
// A double holds every integer up to 2^53 = 9,007,199,254,740,992 exactly, and
// the widths below were derived backwards from that bound rather than picked.
// The LIVE number tops out near 9.9e14 and the CLOSED one near 1.0e13, so both
// sit an order of magnitude inside it.
//
// THE LIVE NUMBER - what this slot is doing on the last bar
//
//   ent     0..999      x 1              entries the open window has cost
//   step    0..9        x 1e3            the ladder rung, 0..7
//   dir     0..2        x 1e4            0 none, 1 short, 2 long
//   fired   0..2        x 1e5            0 nothing last bar, 1 warning, 2 entry
//   wopen   0..1        x 1e6            a window is open, entries or not
//   ago     0..9999     x 1e7            minutes the window has been open
//   streak  0..99       x 1e11           consecutive failures
//   thr     0..99       x 1e13           the threshold they are measured against
//
// wopen is not redundant with ent, and leaving it out was a real hole: a window
// opened by a green diamond has ZERO entries, so an ARMED slot showed nothing at
// all in the grid and counted as quiet. Armed is the state you most want to see
// before it costs anything.
//
// THE CLOSED NUMBER - what this slot last finished
//
//   ago     0..9999     x 1              minutes from its FIRST ENTRY to the close
//   step    0..9        x 1e4            the rung it closed on
//   side    0..2        x 1e5            0 none, 1 short, 2 long
//   ent     0..999      x 1e6            what it cost
//   cnt     0..99       x 1e9            closures so far, wrapping
//   wago    0..9999     x 1e11           minutes from its WINDOW OPENING
//
// TWO AGES, because the row is RANKED by one and DATED by the other, and they
// are different times: with the gate on, a green diamond can stand a quarter of
// an hour before anything is entered.
//
//   wago decides WHICH machine keeps the row when several finish the same win.
//        The chart draws whichever opened first at full strength - see the
//        leader in mar1Gates.pine - so ranking by anything else would leave the
//        two files describing one morning with different machines.
//   ago  is what the Opened column prints, because a closed series is dated from
//        the first thing that cost. maRejection.pine stamps the same time.
//
// cnt is a COUNTER and not a flag, for the reason mar1Scanner.pine gives: a flag
// is true for one bar in the requested symbol's context and there is no promise
// that the bar it is true on is one this side sees. A counter that changed since
// the last reading cannot be missed the same way. The reader tests it with !=
// rather than >, so the wrap costs nothing.
//
// IT WRAPS AT A HUNDRED RATHER THAN AT TEN THOUSAND, and those two digits are
// what paid for wago. A packed double is exact to 2^53, which is 9.007e15, and
// four more digits on top of the old layout would have reached 1e17. Narrowing
// the counter to two digits puts the ceiling at about 1.0e15 - nine times under
// the bound. Nothing is lost: a miss would need exactly one hundred closures on
// one machine of one symbol between two consecutive bars.
//
// The opening TIMES are not sent. They are reconstructed as time_close minus the
// age, in whole minutes, because a millisecond timestamp is 1.7e12 on its own
// and there is no room for one beside the other fields. Minutes are exact on M1
// and M3.
//
// Every field is unpacked SUBTRACT-THEN-DIVIDE: taking the remainder off first
// makes the division land on an integer by construction, rather than landing
// near one and relying on the floor to round the right way.
P_STEP = 1000
P_DIR  = 10000
P_FIRE = 100000
P_OPEN = 1000000
P_AGO  = 10000000
P_STK  = 100000000000
P_THR  = 10000000000000

uEnt(float v)  => int(v) % 1000
uStep(float v) => int((v - int(v) % P_STEP) / P_STEP) % 10
uDir(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % P_DIR) / P_DIR) % 10
uFire(float v) => int((v - int(v) % P_FIRE) / P_FIRE) % 10
uOpen(float v) => int((v - int(v) % P_OPEN) / P_OPEN) % 10
uAgo(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % P_AGO) / P_AGO) % 10000
uStk(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % P_STK) / P_STK) % 100
uThr(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % P_THR) / P_THR)

C_STEP = 10000
C_SIDE = 100000
C_ENT  = 1000000
C_CNT  = 1000000000
C_WAGO = 100000000000

cAgo(float v)  => int(v) % 10000
cStep(float v) => int((v - int(v) % C_STEP) / C_STEP) % 10
cSide(float v) => int((v - int(v) % C_SIDE) / C_SIDE) % 10
cEnt(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % C_ENT) / C_ENT) % 1000
cCnt(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % C_CNT) / C_CNT) % 100
cWAgo(float v) => int((v - int(v) % C_WAGO) / C_WAGO)

// The continuation channel - see ccPk in pairGates. Low to high: the LIVE
// restarted count and the owner slot (plus one, 0 for none), then the last
// continuation CLOSURE - its restarted count, its slot (plus one), a wrapping
// change counter the reader watches, and the takeover's age in whole minutes.
ccLiveN(float v)  => int(v) % 100
ccLiveS(float v)  => int((v - int(v) % 100) / 100) % 10 - 1
ccClsN(float v)   => int((v - int(v) % 1000) / 1000) % 100
ccClsS(float v)   => int((v - int(v) % 100000) / 100000) % 10 - 1
ccClsC(float v)   => int((v - int(v) % 1000000) / 1000000) % 100
ccClsAgo(float v) => int((v - int(v) % 100000000) / 100000000) % 10000

// livePk8's own unpacking - eight base-10 digits, one per slot, each 0..6. See
// LIVE PREVIEW near the foot of the file for what the digit means and why it
// travels as its own small number rather than inside liveVal.
// The flat flag that rides above those eight digits, at 1e8. See MODULE: flat
// filter and livePk8 in pairGates.
livePkFlat(float v8) => int(v8 / 100000000) % 10 == 1

livePkAt(float v8, int slot) =>
    d = slot == 0 ? 1 : slot == 1 ? 10 : slot == 2 ? 100 : slot == 3 ? 1000 :
         slot == 4 ? 10000 : slot == 5 ? 100000 : slot == 6 ? 1000000 : 10000000
    int(v8 / d) % 10

// Every field has a ceiling and every ceiling CLAMPS rather than overflowing.
// These are live readings, not totals: a window three hundred entries deep has
// already told you everything it can, and losing the whole slot over its last
// digit would be the wrong trade.
clamp(int v, int hi) => v < 0 ? 0 : v > hi ? hi : v

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pine Logs - per-entry timestamps, EXPERIMENTAL (user request, 2026-08-11)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// mar1Gates.pine's own version of this writes log.info() OUTSIDE
// request.security, from packed numbers already decoded back in the main
// script - the same route liveVal / closeVal above already take, and the only
// route this file has ever sent anything across that boundary through. A list
// of per-entry timestamps cannot travel that way: only a handful of FIXED
// packed numbers survive request.security, and an open-ended list of entry
// times is not one of them - there is no width to pack it into the way
// closeCnt / closeEnt / closeStep already are.
//
// So this calls log.info() DIRECTLY FROM INSIDE gateOne(), which runs inside
// request.security's own re-evaluated context. Nothing in this project has
// done that before - every other log.info here happens after a request.security
// call already returned, decoding a packed float back in the main script
// (see logPair and the GCLOSE block near the foot). Whether a log line
// written from inside that context actually reaches the Pine Logs panel, and
// whether it is attributed to the right pair, is UNVERIFIED - this needs
// checking against the real chart before it is trusted. If it turns out not
// to work, LOG_ENTRY_TIMES is the one switch to flip back off.
LOG_ENTRY_TIMES = true

slotName(int s) => s == 0 ? 'A1' : s == 1 ? 'A2' : s == 2 ? 'B1' : s == 3 ? 'B2' :
     s == 4 ? 'C1' : s == 5 ? 'C2' : s == 6 ? 'D1' : 'D2'

// Comma separated, 'MM-dd HH:mm' UTC+1 - the same shape mar1Gates.pine's own
// joinTimes uses, so a line from either file reads the same way. Built through
// array.join rather than repeated concatenation - byte-identical output, minus
// the O(n^2) string rebuilding the += spelling paid on long entry lists.
joinTimes(int[] arr) =>
    string s = ''
    if array.size(arr) > 0
        parts = array.new_string(0)
        for i = 0 to array.size(arr) - 1
            array.push(parts, str.format_time(array.get(arr, i), 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'))
        s := array.join(parts, ',')
    s

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One gate, one MA length, on one pair
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// maRejection.pine's machine with the drawing, the statistics and the alerting
// removed, and with the two gate settings as arguments so eight copies can differ
// in them. The same function as gateOne in mar1Gates.pine, returning packed
// numbers instead of a colour - because a colour cannot cross request.security.
//
// THREE VALUES NOW, not two: liveVal and closeVal as before, and a third -
// livePk - for the LIVE PREVIEW. It is a single digit, not a packed field of
// its own, because the caller has to fold eight of these (one per slot) into
// one shared number to keep the request.security tuple small - see livePk8 in
// pairGates and the reasoning at LIVE PREVIEW near the foot of the file.
//
// Everything MA-independent arrives as an argument rather than being recomputed:
// a function called inside request.security evaluates in the REQUESTED symbol's
// context, so the shared block is computed once per pair by pairGates and passed
// down to all eight.
//
// EIGHT CALL SITES ARE WHAT MAKE EIGHT MACHINES. A `var` inside a function
// belongs to the call site rather than to the function, so a loop would have given
// one machine run eight times and one answer.
gateOne(simple int slot, simple int len, float pct, simple int minEnt,
     float srcVal, float atrVal, float bodyRef, float rangeSize,
     float bodySize, float upWick, float dnWick, float tolVal,
     bool isBull, bool isBear, bool wickRejectDown, bool wickRejectUp, bool smallBody,
     bool longWickDown, bool longWickUp, bool counterShort, bool counterLong,
     bool dcShort, bool dcLong, bool hoursOk, bool flatOk, bool active, bool sessionJustEnded) =>
    // switch, NOT the ?: chain this used to be. Pine evaluates EVERY branch of
    // a ternary, so the old spelling computed all four moving averages per
    // machine per bar and kept one - two hundred and forty machines deep. A
    // switch runs only the selected branch, and because maType is a simple
    // input the SAME branch runs on every bar - the series and its history are
    // identical, only the three dead MAs are gone. The compiler may warn about
    // ta.* inside a conditional; that warning is about conditions that change
    // bar to bar, which this one cannot.
    maValue = switch maType
        'WMA' => ta.wma(srcVal, len)
        'EMA' => ta.ema(srcVal, len)
        'RMA' => ta.rma(srcVal, len)
        => ta.sma(srcVal, len)

    // THE TREND TEST, AS A ROLLING SUM RATHER THAN A LOOP (2026-08-12). Same
    // test to the bit - not an approximation - and the same rewrite now lives
    // in maSweepPairs.pine and mar1Gates.pine.
    //
    // It used to be `for i = 1 to trendBars`, reading atrVal[i], close[i] and
    // maValue[i] every iteration: nine history reads at a VARIABLE index per
    // machine per bar. In this file that runs once per slot per pair - eight
    // machines behind each of the request.security calls - so it is the single
    // most repeated piece of arithmetic in the script, and a variable index is
    // the expensive kind: Pine resolves a constant offset at compile time and
    // cannot resolve a loop counter, and has to keep a deeper buffer for every
    // series read that way.
    //
    // The identity: the loop's term at i is
    //
    //     close[i] <= maValue[i] - atrVal[i] * trendSep
    //
    // and every series in it is read at the SAME offset, so the whole term is
    // one expression evaluated i bars ago. The loop is therefore
    // math.sum(term, trendBars) shifted back one bar. atrVal is a function
    // PARAMETER here rather than a global, which changes nothing - a series
    // argument keeps its history across the call, which is what made the old
    // atrVal[i] legal in the first place.
    //
    // The [1] is what makes the range 1..trendBars instead of 0..trendBars-1.
    // Drop it and the test starts including the bar being graded, which is a
    // different strategy.
    //
    // nz() covers the warmup, where math.sum is na until its window fills. The
    // loop reached past the start of history there and compared against na,
    // which is false and counted zero - nz gives the same zero and the same
    // false verdict, so nothing changes at the left edge of the chart.
    sepNow   = atrVal * trendSep
    belowNow = close <= maValue - sepNow ? 1 : 0
    aboveNow = close >= maValue + sepNow ? 1 : 0

    trendDown = nz(math.sum(belowNow, trendBars)[1]) == trendBars
    trendUp   = nz(math.sum(aboveNow, trendBars)[1]) == trendBars

    bodyRejectDown = allowBodyInside and open <= maValue and smallBody and upWick > bodySize
    bodyRejectUp   = allowBodyInside and open >= maValue and smallBody and dnWick > bodySize

    rejectDown = rangeSize > 0 and high >= maValue - tolVal and close <= maValue and (wickRejectDown or bodyRejectDown or longWickDown)
    rejectUp   = rangeSize > 0 and low  <= maValue + tolVal and close >= maValue and (wickRejectUp or bodyRejectUp or longWickUp)

    triggerShort = trendDown and (rejectDown or counterShort or dcShort)
    triggerLong  = trendUp and (rejectUp or counterLong or dcLong)

    var int lastLongBar  = -100000
    var int lastShortBar = -100000
    canFireLong  = bar_index - lastLongBar >= cooldown
    canFireShort = bar_index - lastShortBar >= cooldown

    // flatOk sits beside hoursOk because it is the same kind of veto - "not now"
    // - and it has to land in the same place: on the DETECTED signal, so that a
    // suppressed one never reaches the streak histogram below. It arrives as an
    // argument for the same reason hoursOk does: it belongs to the PAIR, not to
    // this machine, and pairGates measures it once for all eight.
    buyFound  = triggerLong[1] and canFireLong and hoursOk and flatOk
    sellFound = triggerShort[1] and not triggerLong[1] and canFireShort and hoursOk and flatOk

    var int[] failHist    = array.new_int(16, 0)
    var int   curFailRun  = 0
    var int   streak      = 0
    var int   distCache   = -1
    var int   lockedLimit = 0

    gateThreshold = gateMode == 'Off' ? 0 :
         gateMode == 'Manual' ? minEnt :
         gateMode == 'Distribution' ? (distCache > 0 ? distCache : minEnt) :
         math.max(distCache, minEnt)

    gateOk = streak >= gateThreshold

    if streak == 0
        lockedLimit := gateThreshold
    colorLimit = gateMode == 'Off' ? 0 : lockedLimit

    buyTaken     = buyFound and gateOk
    sellTaken    = sellFound and gateOk
    gateArmedNow = gateMode != 'Off' and streak == gateThreshold - 1
    buyWarn      = buyFound and gateArmedNow
    sellWarn     = sellFound and gateArmedNow

    // Read BEFORE the grading below moves the streak, so the step reported for a
    // signal is the rung it ARRIVED on - the same rung the chart paints its
    // marker with. Reading it afterwards would report the rung the signal's own
    // failure created, one step too far up.
    stepAtSignal = ladderStep(streak, colorLimit)

    var int  entriesInWindow = 0
    var bool windowOpen      = false
    var int  windowDir       = 0
    var int  firedNow        = 0
    var int  winOpenTime     = 0

    // TWO TIMESTAMPS, TWO QUESTIONS, and they are genuinely different times: with
    // the gate on, a green diamond can stand several candles before anything is
    // entered. mar1Scanner.pine keeps the same pair for the same reason and the
    // same way round, and this file has to agree with it or one row means two
    // things depending on which scanner drew it.
    //
    //   winOpenTime    when the GATE opened. That is what the live list wants:
    //                  since when has this slot been demanding attention.
    //   firstEntTime   when money first went in. That is what the LOG wants:
    //                  how long did this series actually hold me - and it is the
    //                  time maRejection.pine prints on its success stamp, which
    //                  is what lets a row here and a stamp there be recognised as
    //                  the same series.
    var int  firstEntTime    = 0

    // Every entry this window has cost so far, in order - the EXPERIMENTAL
    // per-entry Pine Logs export (user request, 2026-08-11). See LOG_ENTRY_TIMES
    // above for what this is and the one thing about it that is unverified.
    var int[] entryTimes = array.new_int(0)

    var int closeCnt  = 0
    var int closeEnt  = 0
    var int closeSide = 0
    var int closeStep = 0
    var int closeAgo  = 0

    // The second age - from the GATE OPENING to the close. It is not what the
    // row prints; it is what decides whether the row is this machine's at all
    // when eight of them finish the same win. See the dedup in logPair.
    var int closeWAgo = 0

    if barstate.isconfirmed and active
        firedNow := buyTaken or sellTaken ? 2 : buyWarn or sellWarn ? 1 : 0

        if buyFound
            lastLongBar := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastShortBar := bar_index
        if sellFound
            lastShortBar := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastLongBar := bar_index

        // Doji is a miss on both sides, matching the original exactly.
        if not na(bodyRef) and (buyFound or sellFound)
            hit = (buyFound and isBull) or (sellFound and isBear)
            histChanged = false

            if hit
                curFailRun := 0
            else
                curFailRun += 1
                // ONCE A STREAK REACHES THE CATCH-ALL, NOTHING MOVES ANY MORE - and this
                // used to keep moving it, which is the bug this guard fixes rather than a
                // style choice.
                //
                // The transfer is what keeps a LIVE streak in exactly one bucket: as it grows
                // past n it is added to bucket n and taken back out of n-1. Bucket 15 is a
                // catch-all, so a streak that reaches 15 has ARRIVED and never leaves. With the
                // index clamped by math.min, the sixteenth failure ran the transfer AGAIN -
                // adding a second copy of one live streak to bucket 15 and taking a real one
                // OUT of bucket 14, which went NEGATIVE by one per extra failure.
                //
                // THE TOTAL WAS NEVER AFFECTED, and that is what made it silent for as long as
                // it was: the +1 and the -1 both sit inside the summed range 1..15, so the one
                // number anybody could have checked by hand stayed exactly right while the
                // shape underneath it did not.
                //
                // What it cost: calcIndex walks the cumulative distribution and takes the FIRST
                // crossing of the percentage. A negative bucket 14 drags that cumulative back
                // DOWN, so a crossing that belonged at 14 was missed and picked up at 15
                // instead - one rung too high on the gate threshold, in Distribution and in Max
                // of both. The gate then opens one loss LATER than the distribution says it
                // should. The error is one-sided and bounded: never earlier, and never any
                // bucket other than 14 -> 15.
                //
                // Worked example, 20 consecutive misses against 19 shorter streaks at 95%:
                // bucket 14 reads -4 and bucket 15 reads 6 where they should read 1 and 1, the
                // cumulative falls from 90% to 70% at bucket 14, and the answer comes back 15
                // instead of 14.
                //
                // The same five lines live in SIX files. maRejection.pine is the one where this
                // feeds the gate that decides real entries.
                if curFailRun <= 15
                    array.set(failHist, curFailRun, array.get(failHist, curFailRun) + 1)
                    array.set(failHist, curFailRun - 1, array.get(failHist, curFailRun - 1) - 1)
                    histChanged := true

            streak := hit ? 0 : streak + 1
            // Recomputed only when the histogram itself moved - on a hit, and
            // on a miss already past the catch-all bucket, calcIndex would
            // return exactly what distCache already holds.
            if histChanged
                distCache := calcIndex(failHist, pct)

        buyFailed    = (buyTaken or buyWarn) and not isBull
        sellFailed   = (sellTaken or sellWarn) and not isBear
        greenDiamond = (buyFailed or sellFailed) and colorLimit > 0 and streak == colorLimit

        buySet = successScope == 'All found signals' ? buyFound :
             successScope == 'Entries and warnings' ? (buyTaken or buyWarn) :
             buyTaken
        sellSet = successScope == 'All found signals' ? sellFound :
             successScope == 'Entries and warnings' ? (sellTaken or sellWarn) :
             sellTaken

        win        = (buySet and isBull) or (sellSet and isBear)
        entryTaken = buyTaken or sellTaken

        if entryTaken
            entriesInWindow += 1
            array.push(entryTimes, time)
            windowDir := buyTaken ? 2 : 1
            if entriesInWindow == 1
                firstEntTime := time
            if not windowOpen
                windowOpen := true
                // With the streak gate Off there is no green diamond to open a
                // window, so the first entry is the opening. Timed here rather
                // than at the diamond for exactly that case.
                winOpenTime := time

        if win
            // A window that closed without ever having been opened by a gate or
            // an entry is not a window - it is a win on a slot that was flat, and
            // reporting it would put a zero-entry row in a log about cost.
            if windowOpen or entriesInWindow > 0
                // Wrapping to 1 rather than to 0, so the counter can never land
                // back on the value it holds before the first closure. The reader
                // stores the last value it saw and fires on any change; a wrap to
                // zero would be indistinguishable from a slot that never closed.
                closeCnt  := closeCnt + 1 > 99 ? 1 : closeCnt + 1
                closeEnt  := clamp(entriesInWindow, 999)
                closeSide := buySet and isBull ? 2 : 1
                closeStep := depthStep(entriesInWindow)
                // Measured from the FIRST ENTRY, not from the green diamond, and
                // that is the whole difference between this log and the live
                // list. A window can stand open for a quarter of an hour before
                // anything is entered; dating the row from the diamond made the
                // gates log say 13:49 where mar1Scanner.pine said 14:03 on the
                // same win of the same pair on the same settings, and the second
                // one is the number the project means by a closed series.
                //
                // The fallback is for a window that closed without ever entering
                // - possible when a gate opened on a diamond and the very next
                // signal won. It has no first entry to be dated from.
                closeAgo  := clamp(int((time_close - (firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime :
                     winOpenTime == 0 ? time : winOpenTime)) / 60000), 9999)
                // And the same span measured from the GATE - the green diamond -
                // which is the key the dedup ranks by and the same key
                // mar1Gates.pine ranks its own log and its chart leader by. A
                // window that opened earlier was running while the others joined
                // it, and it is the one both files have to keep.
                closeWAgo := clamp(int((time_close - (winOpenTime > 0 ? winOpenTime : time)) / 60000), 9999)

                // EXPERIMENTAL - log.info() from inside request.security. See
                // LOG_ENTRY_TIMES above. slotOn / active are tested here rather
                // than wrapping the whole block, matching the same guard the
                // rest of this closure already runs under.
                if LOG_ENTRY_TIMES and slotOn(slot) and active
                    log.info('MAR1GSCLOSE;{0};{1};{2};{3};{4};{5};{6}',
                         syminfo.ticker, slotName(slot),
                         str.format_time(firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime : (winOpenTime == 0 ? time : winOpenTime), 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                         str.format_time(time_close, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                         str.tostring(entriesInWindow),
                         buySet and isBull ? 'long' : 'short',
                         joinTimes(entryTimes))
            array.clear(entryTimes)
            windowOpen      := false
            entriesInWindow := 0
            windowDir       := 0
            winOpenTime     := 0
            firstEntTime    := 0
        else if greenDiamond
            // Defensive, not load-bearing - see the identical note in
            // mar1Gates.pine's gateOne(): a fresh window should never find
            // this non-empty, since it is emptied the instant the previous one
            // wins, above.
            array.clear(entryTimes)
            windowOpen      := true
            entriesInWindow := 0
            windowDir       := 0
            winOpenTime     := time
            firstEntTime    := 0

        // The still-open line - "усі ті пари, які знаходяться в ранінгу, і не
        // закриті" (user, 2026-08-11). windowOpen alone, not
        // entriesInWindow > 0, for the same reason mar1Gates.pine's own version
        // reads it that way: a gate ARMED with nothing entered yet is still
        // "not closed" in the literal sense that was asked for.
        //
        // Runs after the win / greenDiamond block above, so a window that just
        // closed this exact bar is correctly absent here - already written as
        // MAR1GSCLOSE instead, once, not twice under two names.
        //
        // AT THE LIVE EDGE ONLY (2026-08-12), and that is a speed fix rather
        // than a change of what the line means. It used to run on EVERY
        // confirmed bar, so a window open for three hundred candles wrote three
        // hundred lines, each one calling joinTimes - a string built by
        // concatenation, with a str.format_time per entry inside it - and all
        // of that ran two hundred and forty times over, inside
        // request.security, across the whole loaded history. String building is
        // the most expensive thing in this file and this was the only place
        // doing it per bar.
        //
        // NOTHING IS LOST, because the question this line answers is "what is
        // open NOW". The historical copies were each superseded by the next bar
        // the instant it confirmed, and an episode that has FINISHED is already
        // in MAR1GSCLOSE with its full entry list. So the surviving line is the
        // only one that was ever read.
        //
        // Two states qualify: the last confirmed bar of history, which is what
        // a chart that has just loaded is standing on, and every confirmed bar
        // once the feed is live, which is where a window opening now has to
        // show up without waiting.
        liveEdge = barstate.islastconfirmedhistory or barstate.isrealtime

        if windowOpen and LOG_ENTRY_TIMES and slotOn(slot) and active and liveEdge
            log.info('MAR1GSOPEN;{0};{1};{2};{3};{4}',
                 syminfo.ticker, slotName(slot),
                 str.format_time(firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime : winOpenTime, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                 str.tostring(entriesInWindow),
                 joinTimes(entryTimes))

        // The session-boundary line - a window still open exactly when this
        // PAIR's hours filter session ends gets logged as if it closed there.
        // LOG ONLY, same as mar1Gates.pine's identical line: windowOpen,
        // entriesInWindow, firstEntTime and entryTimes are untouched below -
        // the machine keeps the window running into tomorrow exactly as it
        // always has.
        if sessionJustEnded and windowOpen and LOG_ENTRY_TIMES and slotOn(slot) and active
            log.info('MAR1GSSESSIONCLOSE;{0};{1};{2};{3};{4};{5}',
                 syminfo.ticker, slotName(slot),
                 str.format_time(firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime : winOpenTime, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                 str.format_time(time, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                 str.tostring(entriesInWindow),
                 joinTimes(entryTimes))

    // The step the table shows is where the machine stands NOW when nothing
    // fired, and the rung the signal arrived on when something did. Those are
    // two different numbers on a bar that graded a failure, and the second one
    // is the one that matches the marker on the chart.
    stepNow = firedNow > 0 ? stepAtSignal : ladderStep(streak, colorLimit)

    // Measured from the bar's OPEN rather than its close, so the age is what has
    // already happened rather than what the forming candle will have cost by the
    // time it finishes. The reader rebuilds the opening stamp as time - ago.
    openAgo = (windowOpen or entriesInWindow > 0) and winOpenTime > 0 ?
         clamp(int((time - winOpenTime) / 60000), 9999) : 0

    liveVal = 1.0 * clamp(gateThreshold, 99) * P_THR + clamp(streak, 99) * P_STK +
         openAgo * P_AGO + (windowOpen ? 1 : 0) * P_OPEN + firedNow * P_FIRE +
         windowDir * P_DIR + stepNow * P_STEP + clamp(entriesInWindow, 999)

    closeVal = 1.0 * closeWAgo * C_WAGO + closeCnt * C_CNT + closeEnt * C_ENT + closeSide * C_SIDE +
         closeStep * C_STEP + closeAgo

    // LIVE PREVIEW - see livePrev in mar1Gates.pine for the full reasoning,
    // reproduced here in numeric-packed form since a request.security boundary
    // cannot carry state any other way. trL/trS below are triggerLong /
    // triggerShort read WITHOUT the [1] offset buyFound / sellFound use -
    // reading THIS bar's own still-forming pattern instead of yesterday's
    // confirmed one. gateThreshold is deliberately NOT projected, for the same
    // reason maRejection.pine's own preview does not project it: it can move
    // by at most one bucket at the close, and simulating the histogram update
    // just to guard against that is a bigger lie than the one it would
    // prevent.
    //
    // THE COOLDOWN IS PROJECTED ONE BAR FORWARD - liveCanLong / liveCanShort,
    // not the raw canFireLong / canFireShort. Reusing those as-is was tried
    // first and was wrong: buyFound / sellFound (the SETTLED signal on this
    // same candle, using triggerLong[1]) have not been graded yet either, but
    // at this candle's own close gateOne stamps lastLongBar / lastShortBar to
    // bar_index the moment either goes through - so a preview built from the
    // OLD lastLongBar could offer an entry the settled pass, one line later,
    // would never have allowed. Projecting the spend forward first, exactly
    // as maRejection.pine's liveLastLong / liveLastShort do, is what keeps the
    // preview from promising a slot the settled marker then withholds.
    liveLastLong  = buyFound or (not perDirectionCooldown and sellFound) ? bar_index : lastLongBar
    liveLastShort = sellFound or (not perDirectionCooldown and buyFound) ? bar_index : lastShortBar
    liveCanLong   = bar_index + 1 - liveLastLong >= cooldown
    liveCanShort  = bar_index + 1 - liveLastShort >= cooldown
    // flatOk is carried into the preview too. Leaving it out here would let the
    // forming candle offer a marker that the settled pass one bar later
    // refuses - the same class of fault the cooldown projection above exists to
    // avoid, and the same one that put a stale IN in the Market column.
    liveFoundL = triggerLong and liveCanLong and hoursOk and flatOk
    liveFoundS = triggerShort and not triggerLong and liveCanShort and hoursOk and flatOk
    liveStreak = (liveFoundL and not isBull) or (liveFoundS and not isBear) ? streak + 1 : (liveFoundL or liveFoundS) ? 0 : streak
    liveGateOk    = liveStreak >= gateThreshold
    liveGateArmed = gateMode != 'Off' and liveStreak == gateThreshold - 1
    liveOn    = active and barstate.isrealtime and not barstate.isconfirmed
    liveBuy   = liveOn and liveFoundL and liveGateOk
    liveSell  = liveOn and liveFoundS and liveGateOk
    liveBuyW  = liveOn and liveFoundL and liveGateArmed
    liveSellW = liveOn and liveFoundS and liveGateArmed

    // 7 WON-BUT-NOT-ARMED LONG, 8 SHORT - a warn signal (streak one short of
    // the gate) that matched the candle it fired on, before the gate ever
    // opened. A win here never becomes an entry (gateOk was false) and never
    // opens a window, so nothing else on the chart or in this table records
    // that it happened - the row this slot might have shown simply reverts to
    // quiet the instant the streak resets (user report, 2026-08-10 - a white
    // marker followed straight by a green one, with no trace of either left
    // behind). [1] rather than a `var`, the same trick buyFound already plays
    // with triggerLong[1]: buyWarn and isBull are both ordinary per-bar
    // locals, so their PREVIOUS bar's values are exactly the outcome of the
    // warn that just closed, and reading them this way holds steady for the
    // whole of the bar now forming without repainting.
    //
    // A LOSING warn is not packed here at all, and that is not an omission -
    // it already leaves its own trace: the streak reaches the gate on that
    // same failure and opens the window, which is the ordinary ARMED state
    // this table already tracks. Giving ARMED its own sustained plate in the
    // Market column, rather than only a row tint, is what makes that trace
    // as visible as this one (2026-08-10).
    warnWonLong  = buyWarn[1] and isBull[1]
    warnWonShort = sellWarn[1] and isBear[1]

    // 5 IN LONG, 6 IN SHORT - added after a real bug, not alongside the
    // preview by design from the start. The Market cell used to read firedNow,
    // which is a `var` written only `if barstate.isconfirmed` - so throughout
    // the whole of the NEXT bar's formation it still held whatever it was
    // stamped with at the PREVIOUS bar's close. The table renders on every
    // realtime tick, so for that entire next bar it kept showing an entry
    // that had already finished one candle ago, on a bar that itself carried
    // no rejection at all - exactly what was flagged as "IN when it isn't."
    //
    // buyTaken and sellTaken are not `var` and are not gated behind
    // isconfirmed - they are the same two unconditional expressions
    // mar1Gates.pine's own inMkt already reads straight from the tuple, which
    // is why the chart never had this fault. Packing them here instead of
    // firedNow is the fix: read fresh every tick, so the moment this bar's own
    // grading resolves - one way or the other - the digit follows it.
    //
    // Ranked ABOVE the preview digits: an actual graded entry is a firmer fact
    // than a pattern still forming, so it wins the one shared digit if both
    // happen to be true on the same tick. The near-miss digits rank BELOW the
    // preview for the same reason in reverse - what the forming candle is
    // doing right now matters more than what a warn did one candle ago.
    livePk = buyTaken ? 5 : sellTaken ? 6 : liveBuy ? 3 : liveSell ? 4 :
         warnWonLong ? 7 : warnWonShort ? 8 :
         liveBuyW ? 1 : liveSellW ? 2 : 0

    [liveVal, closeVal, livePk]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One pair, all eight slots
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The shared block first - everything a moving average does not enter into -
// then eight call sites. Called once per pair through request.security, so each
// of the thirty calls gets its own copy of all eight.
//
// THE FIVE FLAT PARAMETERS ARE WHY THIS FUNCTION TAKES ARGUMENTS AT ALL - it did
// not before. They are declared as inputs down by the pair list rather than up
// here, so they have to arrive as parameters: a free variable referenced inside
// a function body must be declared above the function's own definition line,
// but a parameter only has to be in scope at the CALL SITE. See MODULE: flat
// filter, back near the two-candle module, for why they could not be declared
// here without shifting all sixty pair-list inputs.
pairGates(simple bool flatOn, simple int flatLen, simple float flatLo, simple float flatHi, simple int flatWait) =>
    srcVal = srcName == 'close' ? close :
         srcName == 'open' ? open :
         srcName == 'high' ? high :
         srcName == 'low' ? low :
         srcName == 'hl2' ? hl2 :
         srcName == 'hlc3' ? hlc3 :
         ohlc4

    isBull = open < close
    isBear = open > close

    bodySize  = math.abs(close - open)
    atrVal    = ta.atr(atrLen)
    bodyRef   = ta.sma(bodySize, refLen)[1]
    rangeSize = high - low
    upWick    = high - math.max(open, close)
    dnWick    = math.min(open, close) - low
    upWickRef = ta.sma(upWick, refLen)[1]
    dnWickRef = ta.sma(dnWick, refLen)[1]

    // Resolved inside the requested context, so a JPY cross gets its own pip.
    pipSizeSym = syminfo.mintick * (syminfo.type == 'forex' ? 10.0 : 1.0)
    tolVal     = atrVal * touchTol

    wickRejectDown = upWick >= wickRatio * rangeSize and upWick >= minPips * pipSizeSym
    wickRejectUp   = dnWick >= wickRatio * rangeSize and dnWick >= minPips * pipSizeSym
    smallBody      = not na(bodyRef) and bodySize < bodyRef
    longWickDown   = allowLongWick and not na(upWickRef) and upWick > upWickRef
    longWickUp     = allowLongWick and not na(dnWickRef) and dnWick > dnWickRef

    counterShort = allowCounterCandle and isBull and isBear[1]
    counterLong  = allowCounterCandle and isBear and isBull[1]

    dcRef   = DC_REF == 'Open' ? open[DC_BACK] : close[DC_BACK]
    dcReady = DC_ON and not na(dcRef)
    dcShort = dcReady and isBull and isBull[1] and isBear[2] and close <= dcRef
    dcLong  = dcReady and isBear and isBear[1] and isBull[2] and close >= dcRef

    // UTC+1, the frame the hours filter and every timestamp in this project use.
    localHour = (hour + 1) % 24
    inHours   = localHour >= startHour and localHour < endHour
    hoursOk   = not restrictToHours or inHours

    // Whether THIS PAIR's session just closed on this bar - see the identical
    // flag in mar1Gates.pine for the full reasoning. Computed here, per pair,
    // because inHours itself is per pair in this file (each pair keeps its own
    // bar clock inside request.security); mar1Gates.pine has one copy because
    // it has one symbol.
    sessionJustEnded = restrictToHours and not inHours and inHours[1]

    // THE FLAT VERDICT FOR THIS PAIR - one per pair, not one per machine. See
    // MODULE: flat filter at the top of the file for what it measures and why.
    // It is computed here, in the shared block, precisely because it reads no
    // moving average: all eight gates below take the same bool.
    //
    // na for the first flatLen bars, because math.sum propagates na - and na
    // fails BOTH comparisons below, so the state simply stays where it is
    // during warmup rather than reading a zero ratio as a flat.
    flatPath = math.sum(math.abs(close - close[1]), flatLen)
    flatEr   = nz(flatPath) > 0 ? math.abs(close - close[flatLen]) / flatPath : float(na)

    var bool flatNow  = false
    var int  flatLeft = -100000

    // Confirmed bars only. The verdict has to hold still for the whole of a
    // forming candle, or the signal read from that candle changes under the
    // reader - the same rule the closed log is written under.
    enteredThisBar = false
    if barstate.isconfirmed
        if flatNow
            if flatEr > flatHi
                flatNow  := false
                flatLeft := bar_index
        else if flatEr < flatLo
            flatNow := true
            enteredThisBar := true

    // The bar flat turns on does not stand aside for its OWN close - only
    // from the next bar. A signal graded this bar reads triggerLong[1], a
    // pattern that completed on the PREVIOUS bar, and the live preview held
    // the not-yet-flat verdict stable through this whole bar's formation
    // (flatNow never changes mid-candle, only right here at confirmation) -
    // so grading this same bar against the flat state its own close just
    // produced contradicted what the preview had been showing the whole
    // time it formed (user report, 2026-08-10, in mar1Gates.pine,
    // propagated here since these five inputs and this same block must
    // stay identical across all six files). Leaving flat is unaffected -
    // flatWait already owns how soon trading resumes after that, and 0 has
    // always meant the breakout candle itself; only entering is deferred.
    flatHold = (flatNow and not enteredThisBar) or bar_index - flatLeft < flatWait
    flatOk   = not (flatOn and flatHold)

    active = scanBars <= 0 or bar_index > last_bar_index - scanBars

    // GENERATED - eight call sites and the tuple they feed.


    [q1, k1, p1] = gateOne(0, lenA1, pctA, minA, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q2, k2, p2] = gateOne(1, lenA2, pctA, minA, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q3, k3, p3] = gateOne(2, lenB1, pctB, minB, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q4, k4, p4] = gateOne(3, lenB2, pctB, minB, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q5, k5, p5] = gateOne(4, lenC1, pctC, minC, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q6, k6, p6] = gateOne(5, lenC2, pctC, minC, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q7, k7, p7] = gateOne(6, lenD1, pctD, minD, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)
    [q8, k8, p8] = gateOne(7, lenD2, pctD, minD, srcVal, atrVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, active, sessionJustEnded)

    // Eight single digits (0..8, since the near-miss codes 7/8 joined 0..6)
    // folded into one small number rather than returned as an eight-value
    // tuple, so the request.security signature below grows by ONE field
    // instead of eight - the packed LIVE numbers already sit close to their
    // own ceiling and this stays well clear of it, at most 88,888,888.
    //
    // And ONE MORE DIGIT on top, at 1e8: whether this pair is standing aside for
    // a flat right now. It rides here rather than in liveVal because it belongs
    // to the PAIR and not to any one machine - eight copies of one bit would be
    // seven copies too many - and because this number has room to spare where
    // liveVal does not: eight digits of 0..8 top out at 88,888,888, so the flag
    // lands clear of everything below it and the whole thing stays under 2e8.
    livePk8 = 1.0 * p1 + p2 * 10 + p3 * 100 + p4 * 1000 + p5 * 10000 + p6 * 100000 + p7 * 1000000 + p8 * 10000000 +
         (flatHold and flatOn ? 100000000 : 0)

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE TAPE LOCK AND THE SECOND WIND, per pair (user rule, 2026-08-20)
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // mar1Gates.pine's rule, computed in the pair's own context from the
    // same packed digits the grid reads: one slot OWNS the pair from its
    // first entry to the close of its window, and when the tape frees and
    // a window that was ALREADY OPEN takes it, the entries from the
    // takeover on are counted again from one - the real cost of the
    // CONTINUED trade. That restarted count is what the small cross on
    // the gates chart carries, and what travels below as ccPk: live for
    // the grid and the list, stamped at the closure for the log.
    var int  wLockS = -1
    var bool wContOn = false
    var int  wContN = 0
    var int  wPrevClose = 0
    var int  wTakeT = 0
    var int  wClsN = 0
    var int  wClsS = -1
    var int  wClsC = 0
    var int  wClsAgo = 0

    if wLockS >= 0
        qOwn = wLockS == 0 ? q1 : wLockS == 1 ? q2 : wLockS == 2 ? q3 : wLockS == 3 ? q4 :
             wLockS == 4 ? q5 : wLockS == 5 ? q6 : wLockS == 6 ? q7 : q8
        if not (slotOn(wLockS) and (uOpen(qOwn) == 1 or uFire(qOwn) == 2))
            // The owner's window closed, or its gate was unticked. A
            // CONTINUATION closing is the event the log's cross exists
            // for: stamp its restarted count, its slot and the takeover's
            // age, and step the counter the reader watches.
            if wContOn
                wClsN := clamp(wContN, 99)
                wClsS := wLockS
                wClsAgo := clamp(int((time_close - wTakeT) / 60000), 9999)
                wClsC := (wClsC + 1) % 100
            wLockS := -1
            wPrevClose := time
            wContOn := false
            wContN := 0
        else if wContOn and uFire(qOwn) == 2
            wContN += 1
    if wLockS < 0
        // The latch - the first entry takes the pair. Oldest window first
        // on a shared candle, the higher slot on an exact tie: uAgo is
        // minutes since the window opened, so oldest = largest, and >=
        // keeps the later slot, exactly as the active-slot pre-pass does.
        int fAge = -1
        for wSl = 0 to 7
            if slotOn(wSl)
                wQv = wSl == 0 ? q1 : wSl == 1 ? q2 : wSl == 2 ? q3 : wSl == 3 ? q4 :
                     wSl == 4 ? q5 : wSl == 5 ? q6 : wSl == 6 ? q7 : q8
                if uFire(wQv) == 2 and uAgo(wQv) >= fAge
                    fAge := uAgo(wQv)
                    wLockS := wSl
        if wLockS >= 0
            // Already open before the tape last freed = a continuation.
            // The opening minute is rebuilt the way every reader here
            // rebuilds one - the bar's own stamp minus the age - and a
            // window opened on the freeing bar itself carries that bar's
            // time, not smaller, so it starts fresh.
            wContOn := wPrevClose > 0 and time - fAge * 60000 < wPrevClose
            wContN := wContOn ? 1 : 0
            wTakeT := time

            // ENTERED AND CLOSED ON THE SAME BAR - the one-entry win, the
            // commonest continuation there is. A signal fires and grades on
            // one candle, so by the time this bar is packed the window is
            // already gone: uOpen reads 0 on the very bar uFire reads 2,
            // and openAgo was reset with it - which is why the test above
            // just called this ownership fresh. The CLOSED channel is
            // stamping this exact closure though, and its cWAgo still
            // carries the gate-opening age the dedup ranks by, so the
            // continuation verdict is retaken from there - and the closure
            // is stamped HERE: the release branch above runs on a LATER bar
            // than the h-channel counter this closure steps on, and the
            // log's reader only looks at this channel on that same bar.
            qNew = wLockS == 0 ? q1 : wLockS == 1 ? q2 : wLockS == 2 ? q3 : wLockS == 3 ? q4 :
                 wLockS == 4 ? q5 : wLockS == 5 ? q6 : wLockS == 6 ? q7 : q8
            if uOpen(qNew) == 0
                kNew = wLockS == 0 ? k1 : wLockS == 1 ? k2 : wLockS == 2 ? k3 : wLockS == 3 ? k4 :
                     wLockS == 4 ? k5 : wLockS == 5 ? k6 : wLockS == 6 ? k7 : k8
                wContOn := wPrevClose > 0 and time_close - cWAgo(kNew) * 60000 < wPrevClose
                if wContOn
                    // Entered once and closed - the restarted count IS one.
                    wClsN := 1
                    wClsS := wLockS
                    wClsAgo := clamp(int((time_close - wTakeT) / 60000), 9999)
                    wClsC := (wClsC + 1) % 100
                wLockS := -1
                wPrevClose := time
                wContOn := false
                wContN := 0

    ccPk = 1.0 * clamp(wContOn ? wContN : 0, 99) + (wLockS + 1) * 100 +
         wClsN * 1000 + (wClsS + 1) * 100000 + wClsC * 1000000 + wClsAgo * 100000000

    [q1, q2, q3, q4, q5, q6, q7, q8, k1, k2, k3, k4, k5, k6, k7, k8, livePk8, ccPk]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pairs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Thirty of the forty request calls TradingView allows, which is what fixes the
// scanner to one timeframe: sixty would be needed for both, and there are forty.
//
// Blank or unticked means "skip this slot" - it still spends a call, because
// Pine cannot make one conditional, but it is pointed at the chart's own symbol,
// which is already loaded, and its numbers are thrown away.
//
// The defaults are the same watchlist mar1Scanner.pine ships with, in the same
// order, so the two files describe the same set of pairs out of the box.
gList1 = 'Pairs 1-10'
gList2 = 'Pairs 11-20'
gList3 = 'Pairs 21-30'

use01 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 1', inline = 'p01', group = gList1, display = display.none,
     tooltip = 'The tick is the off switch. Each ticked slot is eight state machines over that pair\'s whole history, so this is also the main performance lever.')
sym01 = input.symbol('FX:EURUSD', '', inline = 'p01', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use02 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 2', inline = 'p02', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym02 = input.symbol('FX:GBPUSD', '', inline = 'p02', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use03 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 3', inline = 'p03', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym03 = input.symbol('FX:USDCAD', '', inline = 'p03', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use04 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 4', inline = 'p04', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym04 = input.symbol('FX:USDCHF', '', inline = 'p04', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use05 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 5', inline = 'p05', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym05 = input.symbol('FX:USDJPY', '', inline = 'p05', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use06 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 6', inline = 'p06', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym06 = input.symbol('FX:AUDUSD', '', inline = 'p06', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use07 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 7', inline = 'p07', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym07 = input.symbol('FX:EURGBP', '', inline = 'p07', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use08 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 8', inline = 'p08', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym08 = input.symbol('FX:EURCHF', '', inline = 'p08', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use09 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 9', inline = 'p09', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym09 = input.symbol('FX:EURJPY', '', inline = 'p09', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use10 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 10', inline = 'p10', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym10 = input.symbol('FX:EURAUD', '', inline = 'p10', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use11 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 11', inline = 'p11', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym11 = input.symbol('FX:EURCAD', '', inline = 'p11', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use12 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 12', inline = 'p12', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym12 = input.symbol('FX:GBPAUD', '', inline = 'p12', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use13 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 13', inline = 'p13', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym13 = input.symbol('FX:GBPJPY', '', inline = 'p13', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use14 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 14', inline = 'p14', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym14 = input.symbol('FX:GBPCHF', '', inline = 'p14', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use15 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 15', inline = 'p15', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym15 = input.symbol('FX:GBPCAD', '', inline = 'p15', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use16 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 16', inline = 'p16', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym16 = input.symbol('FX:CHFJPY', '', inline = 'p16', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use17 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 17', inline = 'p17', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym17 = input.symbol('FX:CADCHF', '', inline = 'p17', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use18 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 18', inline = 'p18', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym18 = input.symbol('FX:CADJPY', '', inline = 'p18', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use19 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 19', inline = 'p19', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym19 = input.symbol('FX:AUDJPY', '', inline = 'p19', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use20 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 20', inline = 'p20', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym20 = input.symbol('FX:AUDCHF', '', inline = 'p20', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use21 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 21', inline = 'p21', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym21 = input.symbol('FX:AUDCAD', '', inline = 'p21', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use22 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 22', inline = 'p22', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym22 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p22', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use23 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 23', inline = 'p23', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym23 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p23', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use24 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 24', inline = 'p24', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym24 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p24', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use25 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 25', inline = 'p25', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym25 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p25', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use26 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 26', inline = 'p26', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym26 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p26', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use27 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 27', inline = 'p27', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym27 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p27', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use28 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 28', inline = 'p28', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym28 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p28', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use29 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 29', inline = 'p29', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym29 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p29', group = gList3, display = display.none)
use30 = input.bool(false, 'Pair 30', inline = 'p30', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym30 = input.symbol('', '', inline = 'p30', group = gList3, display = display.none)

// request.security refuses an empty symbol, so a blank slot is pointed at the
// chart itself and filtered out later by the same blank test.
//
// Written out as plain ternaries rather than through a helper on purpose. The
// symbol argument must be a SIMPLE string, and Pine infers the qualifier of a
// user function's return value rather than taking it from the arguments - a
// helper here would very likely come back as series and be refused.
s01 = use01 and sym01 != '' ? sym01 : syminfo.tickerid
s02 = use02 and sym02 != '' ? sym02 : syminfo.tickerid
s03 = use03 and sym03 != '' ? sym03 : syminfo.tickerid
s04 = use04 and sym04 != '' ? sym04 : syminfo.tickerid
s05 = use05 and sym05 != '' ? sym05 : syminfo.tickerid
s06 = use06 and sym06 != '' ? sym06 : syminfo.tickerid
s07 = use07 and sym07 != '' ? sym07 : syminfo.tickerid
s08 = use08 and sym08 != '' ? sym08 : syminfo.tickerid
s09 = use09 and sym09 != '' ? sym09 : syminfo.tickerid
s10 = use10 and sym10 != '' ? sym10 : syminfo.tickerid
s11 = use11 and sym11 != '' ? sym11 : syminfo.tickerid
s12 = use12 and sym12 != '' ? sym12 : syminfo.tickerid
s13 = use13 and sym13 != '' ? sym13 : syminfo.tickerid
s14 = use14 and sym14 != '' ? sym14 : syminfo.tickerid
s15 = use15 and sym15 != '' ? sym15 : syminfo.tickerid
s16 = use16 and sym16 != '' ? sym16 : syminfo.tickerid
s17 = use17 and sym17 != '' ? sym17 : syminfo.tickerid
s18 = use18 and sym18 != '' ? sym18 : syminfo.tickerid
s19 = use19 and sym19 != '' ? sym19 : syminfo.tickerid
s20 = use20 and sym20 != '' ? sym20 : syminfo.tickerid
s21 = use21 and sym21 != '' ? sym21 : syminfo.tickerid
s22 = use22 and sym22 != '' ? sym22 : syminfo.tickerid
s23 = use23 and sym23 != '' ? sym23 : syminfo.tickerid
s24 = use24 and sym24 != '' ? sym24 : syminfo.tickerid
s25 = use25 and sym25 != '' ? sym25 : syminfo.tickerid
s26 = use26 and sym26 != '' ? sym26 : syminfo.tickerid
s27 = use27 and sym27 != '' ? sym27 : syminfo.tickerid
s28 = use28 and sym28 != '' ? sym28 : syminfo.tickerid
s29 = use29 and sym29 != '' ? sym29 : syminfo.tickerid
s30 = use30 and sym30 != '' ? sym30 : syminfo.tickerid

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Flat filter
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Declared here, after the thirty pair slots and before the request.security
// calls that need them, rather than up by MODULE: flat filter near the top.
// TradingView maps a saved setting onto an input by POSITION, and thirty pair
// slots - sixty inputs - sit between here and there; declaring these five above
// them would rotate the entire watchlist on any chart already carrying this
// script. Here they cost nobody anything. pairGates() takes them as PARAMETERS
// so its own definition, well above the pair list, never needs them declared
// before it - see the note on its signature.
//
// These five must hold the same values in maRejection.pine, mar1Gates.pine,
// mar1GatesScanner.pine, mar1Scanner.pine, maSweep.pine and maSweepPairs.pine.
// Nothing checks that for you.
gFlat = 'Flat filter'

// Left VISIBLE, like the streak gate and the hours filter: with it on, a
// stretch of candles carrying no markers means 'nothing was allowed to fire'
// rather than 'nothing triggered', and reading the grid without knowing which
// is reading fiction.
flatOn = input.bool(true, 'Stand aside while flat', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'Suppresses the DETECTED signal, in exactly the place the hours filter does. An open window keeps its entries and its rung and stands still until the flat ends - the ladder is deferred, never abandoned - and the streak does not move while it is paused. Untick to get the behaviour this file had before the filter existed.')
flatLen = input.int(8, 'Bars measured', minval = 2, maxval = 500, group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'How far back the efficiency ratio looks: net displacement over these bars divided by the path actually walked. Shorter reacts sooner and calls more stretches flat; longer only notices the long ones.', display = display.none)
flatLo = input.float(0.2, 'Enter flat below  /  leave above', minval = 0.01, maxval = 1.0, step = 0.01, inline = 'fl', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'Two thresholds, and the second must be the higher of the two. The state enters flat below the first and leaves only above the second - with a single threshold the verdict flickers bar by bar around it and windows end up half blocked, which is worse than no filter at all. On the ratio itself, 1.0 is a straight line and 0.0 is a walk that ended where it started.', display = display.none)
flatHi = input.float(0.45, '', minval = 0.01, maxval = 1.0, step = 0.01, inline = 'fl', group = gFlat, display = display.none)
flatWait = input.int(0, 'Bars to keep standing aside after it ends', minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'The candle that breaks a range is exactly the wick-rejection candle this strategy likes, and it is also the one most often taken straight back. 0 resumes on the breakout candle itself.', display = display.none)

// ignore_invalid_symbol keeps one mistyped ticker from killing the whole script;
// that slot comes back na and is counted as unresolved in the footer.
//
// GENERATED - thirty destructurings of eight live and eight closed values each.
[g01_1, g01_2, g01_3, g01_4, g01_5, g01_6, g01_7, g01_8, h01_1, h01_2, h01_3, h01_4, h01_5, h01_6, h01_7, h01_8, lp01, cc01] = request.security(s01, timeframe.period, pairGates(flatOn, flatLen, flatLo, flatHi, flatWait), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[g02_1, g02_2, g02_3, g02_4, g02_5, g02_6, g02_7, g02_8, h02_1, h02_2, h02_3, h02_4, h02_5, h02_6, h02_7, h02_8, lp02, cc02] = request.security(s02, timeframe.period, pairGates(flatOn, flatLen, flatLo, flatHi, flatWait), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[g03_1, g03_2, g03_3, g03_4, g03_5, g03_6, g03_7, g03_8, h03_1, h03_2, h03_3, h03_4, h03_5, h03_6, h03_7, h03_8, lp03, cc03] = request.security(s03, timeframe.period, pairGates(flatOn, flatLen, flatLo, flatHi, flatWait), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
